I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the reusable diffraction utilities. Then I’ll build a chemistry-based candidate inventory, health-screen every serious CIF source, and only retain phases that survive profile replay and source challenges.

The chemistry is a Pb–W–O reaction at 500 °C, so the initial exact-formula watch list is PbWO₄ (including its distinct polymorphs), residual WO₃/Pb₃O₄, and charge-balanced lead-rich or tungsten-rich oxides. No precursor amounts or ratio are present in the metadata, so I will not impose a mass-balance ratio that was not reported. I’m now mapping the observed peak families and the locally available COD/GSAS-II interfaces.

The peak map already separates two width regimes: a broad dominant family (for example 27.81°, 45.13°, 55.63°, 72.36°) and several much sharper low-angle lines (23.53°, 24.02°, 24.84°). That is evidence for at least two crystalline components, not merely a single isotropically broadened phase. Before any profile fitting, I’m health-screening the distinct PbWO₄ records and the plausible precursor/oxide records as required.

The exact source challenge is now well anchored: the broad family matches scheelite-like Pb–W–O positions, while the sharp triplet and its independent 33–35° and 49–56° families match monoclinic WO₃. I’m moving from stick-pattern matching to whole-profile tests, starting each source from the same neutral checkpoint so the very similar scheelite PbWO₄ and Pb-deficient superstructure cannot be selected by the dominant peaks alone.

The first valid replay gives wR = 9.705% with nonnegative profile terms (X = 8.60, U = W = 0) and a 0.067 µm broad-domain size, versus 13.45% at the neutral-size checkpoint. WO₃ retains a positive scale. I’ve rejected the earlier numerically lower-looking route because U and W crossed below zero; that bound-crossing comparison is invalid under the requested protocol. Next I’m challenging the Pb–W–O source/topology at the same neutral and elaborated checkpoints.

The PbWO₄ topology challenge is decisive for raspite (wR ≈ 26.72%, rejected), but scheelite PbWO₄ and Pb₇W₈O₂₈.₈ remain very close after broadening: 9.6945% versus 9.7048%. Scheelite is also better at the neutral checkpoint (13.2445% versus 13.4497%) with fewer structural complications, so it currently leads, but I’m treating the 0.01-point elaborated gap as potentially nuisance-coupled and will cross-seed/replay it. I’m now ranking distinct WO₃ polymorph records against that common scheelite model.

The residual inventory reveals a third phase, not just a profile defect. The large unmatched families at 26.81°, 31.22°, 32.55°, 47–52°, 58.48°, and 66.57° all map coherently to Pb₃O₄ after the same angular correction; several are independent and non-coincident. This is unreacted precursor chemistry and meets the retention gate far more strongly than a weak-phase hypothesis. I’m adding Pb₃O₄ from a neutral checkpoint and comparing its tetragonal and orthorhombic records before allowing phase broadening.

Pb₃O₄ is strongly retained: the health-passing tetragonal record lowers wR from 9.69% to 7.51% even with neutral phase width and to 6.80% with positive size broadening; its refined crystalline fraction is about 17%. The orthorhombic physical competitor remains much worse (9.39%), and one alternate record imported chemically invalid atom types, so it is excluded. I’m now resetting all three phases to genuinely neutral width terms and running size/strain/combined challenges from the same saved checkpoint.

The first width loop exposed an important coupling: when the common X term was allowed to broaden all phases to X≈19.85, several strain refinements became negative, and WO₃ size hit the neutral boundary with singularity. Those comparisons are invalid and will not be advanced. I’m reloading the accepted X≈8.8 profile from the three-phase fit, keeping the sharp WO₃ width neutral, and challenging positive strain only as a complement to the already positive size terms for the two broad phases.

The complementary strain test also failed physically: Lorentzian strain crossed negative, and Gaussian strain either crossed negative or stalled without a replay-stable gain. The accepted width model is therefore isotropic size for scheelite PbWO₄ and Pb₃O₄, with WO₃ neutral; this is the simpler replay-stable model. I’m now testing smooth background order and the shared high-angle intensity trend through polarization and surface roughness, while tracking whether fractions remain stable.

The background/intensity replay is still running from the shared checkpoint. Once it finishes, I’ll keep only nuisance terms that are physical, replay-stable, and do not shift the phase fractions materially.

The shared-intensity trials exposed a useful boundary: polarization drove essentially to its physical limit (~0.995), while the joint polarization/roughness fit went nonphysical (polarization >1, negative roughness). I’m treating that joint result as invalid and will challenge the remaining physical roughness model against simple texture and a fixed-boundary replay rather than accepting the lower residual at face value.

The family-specific checks are now discriminating cleanly: PbWO₄ has replay-stable uniaxial size broadening (about 0.081 µm perpendicular to [001] versus 0.061 µm along it), improving Rwp from 6.804% to 6.780%. Pb₃O₄ anisotropy and all tested one-axis texture terms give only marginal gains; I’m now replaying those terms after the physical roughness correction to see whether they survive the shared-intensity change.

The shared nuisance loop is stable from very different starts: the one-parameter roughness term converges to 0.1651 both times. A two-parameter roughness model remains highly correlated and drifts, so it is rejected. PbWO₄ [100] March–Dollase texture survives the roughness replay with a modest ratio of 1.050 and stable fractions; Pb₃O₄ uniaxial width also remains positive. I’m combining only those supported terms, then rerunning the close source-record competitors from that checkpoint.

The first mature control replay reproduced the accepted result exactly (Rwp 5.078%). The alternate scheelite record then entered a poor local minimum from its literature cell, so I stopped that batch rather than count an invalid comparison. I’m cross-seeding each competitor with its independently refined cell and restarting the source challenge, as required for a fair ranking.

The mature source replay is decisive. The retained records reproduce the checkpoint at Rwp 5.078%; the nearest competitors give 5.317% for Pb₇W₈O₂₈.₈, 5.458% for alternate monoclinic WO₃, and 5.152% for alternate tetragonal Pb₃O₄. Lower-symmetry WO₃ and orthorhombic Pb₃O₄ are much worse, with the latter also collapsing an axial size and distorting its cell. I’m now auditing angular residuals and isolated peak families before freezing the project.

The profile audit supports the retained terms: isolated WO₃ triplet positions agree within 0.005–0.007° and FWHM within about 0.008°, while representative PbWO₄ low/mid/high families agree in position within 0.014°; the largest remaining mismatch is high-angle PbWO₄ width/asymmetry. I’m freezing the conservative eight-term-background model over every measured point and performing the required save–reopen–replay now.

The full-range project converged identically after reopening (maximum shift/esd 0.001). Final Rwp is 5.0718%, and all three exported CIFs pass the required geometry/occupancy health screen. I’m doing the final cross-file consistency check now and will record the source discrimination and realistic fraction uncertainty in the report.

The structural/result checks passed; the consistency script only tripped on the CSV’s decimal serialization tolerance. I’m quantifying that rounding difference and will regenerate at higher precision if needed so `fit.csv` numerically replays the project exactly.

Completed the phase analysis and full-range GSAS-II refinement.

- PbWO₄ (scheelite): 60.60 wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic): 21.98 wt%
- Pb₃O₄ (tetragonal): 17.42 wt%

Final fit: Rwp 5.0718%, Rp 3.3918%, GOF 2.0441. Fractions sum to 1.0 over modeled crystalline phases. All final CIFs passed the geometry/occupancy health screen. Practical fraction uncertainty is approximately 1–2 wt% absolute; amorphous content cannot be quantified.

